        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change adds `membershipNumber` as a derived-field expression in `OwnerMapper.toOwnerDto`, directly alongside the two existing derived fields (`displayName`, `initials`), and this is the right unit for it.\n\nWhy this is the correct placement (and not a function class like `AssignCustomerCode`):\n- `customerCode` lives in a write-time function class because it is a *persisted*, sequence-dependent value \u2014 it depends on the owner count at creation time and cannot be re-derived later (`AssignCustomerCode.java:16-21`).\n- `membershipNumber` is a *pure projection* of the owner's own already-persisted fields (`customerCode` + `registrationDate`). The acceptance-test comment even notes it is \"Derived from the owner's own fields, so seed-independent.\" A read-only projection belongs in the DTO mapper, not in a persistence pipeline step. The engineer correctly resisted mirroring the `customerCode` treatment.\n\nOther structural checks:\n- Expression complexity is in line with the neighboring `initials` mapping \u2014 no new idiom introduced, and the `String.format(\"%02d\", \u2026)` reads naturally.\n- No duplication; the mapper now holds three cohesive derived-field mappings and is not drifting toward a catch-all.\n- The `openapi.yml` schema entry sits next to `customerCode` with matching `readOnly`, `example`, and description style, so the DTO contract stays coherent.\n\nLogic is in the right place, nothing is duplicated, and a maintainer of this codebase would find it natural."
